# Streaming pages

A screen with several independent reads paints a shell after the one query it needs (or immediately, when the URL already decides). Each visual section is an async Server Component inside `<Suspense fallback={<SectionFallback title="..." />}>` from `@/shared/ui`.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — the page stays blank until every read finishes
const [rows, grants] = await Promise.all([listRoles(id), listGrants(id)]);
return <RolesPanel grants={grants} rows={rows} />;

// ✅ GOOD — roles and grants fill in on their own
return (
  <>
    <Suspense fallback={<SectionFallback title="Tenant roles" />}>
      <RolesListSlot employerId={id} />
    </Suspense>
    <Suspense fallback={<SectionFallback title="Page grants" />}>
      <RoleGrantsSlot employerId={id} selectedRoleId={roleId} />
    </Suspense>
  </>
);
```

- A tabbed screen loads only the active tab.
- One visual unit may `Promise.all` its own reads inside its boundary. A later section that needs an earlier result is a nested boundary.
- Do not wrap the whole page in one `Suspense`.
- `"use client"` files stay interactive islands. They do not fetch the section's data.
